## 2.1.1.1. The `for` Loop

The `for` loop is a common loop construct in C. It is used to repeat a block of code a specified number of times. The syntax for the `for` loop is:

```c
for (initialization; condition; increment) {
    // code to repeat
}
```

The `initialization` statement is executed once before the loop starts. The `condition` statement is evaluated before each iteration of the loop. If the condition is true, the code in the loop is executed. If the condition is false, the loop terminates and control passes to the statement after the loop. The `increment` statement is executed after each iteration of the loop.

The following example shows a simple `for` loop that prints the numbers from 1 to 10:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    int i;

    for (i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
        printf("%d\n", i);
    }
}
```

The `for` loop can also be used to iterate over arrays or other data structures. The following example iterates over an array of integers:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    int numbers[5] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    int i;

    for (i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
        printf("%d\n", numbers[i]);
    }
}
```

## 2.1.1.2. The `while` Loop

The `while` loop is another common loop construct in C. It is used to repeat a block of code while a specified condition is true. The syntax for the `while` loop is:

```c
while (condition) {
    // code to repeat
}
```

The `condition` statement is evaluated before each iteration of the loop. If the condition is true, the code in the loop is executed. If the condition is false, the loop terminates and control passes to the statement after the loop.

The following example shows a simple `while` loop that prints the numbers from 1 to 10:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    int i = 1;

    while (i <= 10) {
        printf("%d\n", i);
        i++;
    }
}
```

The `while` loop can also be used to iterate over arrays or other data structures. The following example iterates over an array of integers:

```c
