## 4. Structures and Arrays

Structures and arrays are two of the most common data types in C.

### 4.1 Structures

Structures are a way of grouping related data together. They are defined using the keyword `struct`.

```c
struct person {
    char name[100];
    int status;
};
```

This defines a structure called `person` with two members: a `char` array called `name` and an `int` called `status`.

To access a member of a structure, you use the dot operator.

```c
struct person p;

strcpy(p.name, "John");
p.status = 0;
```

This code sets the name to "John" and the status to 0.

### 4.2 Arrays

Arrays are a way of storing a fixed number of similar data types. They are defined using the keyword `array`.

```c
int numbers[5];
```

This defines an array called `numbers` with 5 elements.

To access an element of an array, you use the subscript operator.

```c
numbers[0] = 10;
numbers[1] = 20;
numbers[2] = 30;
numbers[3] = 40;
numbers[4] = 50;
```

This code sets the first element of the array to 10, the second element to 20, and so on.

### 4.3 Pointers

Pointers are a way of storing the address of a variable. They are defined using the keyword `pointer`.

```c
int *p;
```

This defines a pointer called `p`.

To access the value stored at a pointer, you use the arrow operator.

```c
*p = 10;
```

This code sets the value stored at the pointer to 10.

