## Reading from a file

Reading from a file is similar to writing to a file, but with a few differences. First, the file must be opened in read mode, and the `fscanf()` function must be used instead of `fprintf()`. Second, the `fscanf()` function returns the number of items read, which can be used to determine if the entire file was read.

Here is an example of reading from a file:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <limits.h>

#define FILENAME "data.csv"

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    // open file
    FILE *fp = fopen(FILENAME, "r");
    if (fp == NULL) {
        printf("Error opening file!\n");
        return 1;
    }

    // read from file
    float a, b, c;
    int num_items_read = fscanf(fp, "%f, %f, %f", &a, &b, &c);
    if (num_items_read!= 3) {
        printf("Error reading from file!\n");
        return 1;
    }

    // print results
    printf("a = %f\n", a);
    printf("b = %f\n", b);
    printf("c = %f\n", c);

    // close file
    fclose(fp);

    return 0;
}
